{The following is an actual blitz game. Since the final position looks impossible, I have included the entire game as proof.} 
1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Qc2 dxc4 5. Qxc4 Bf5 6. g3 e6 7. Bg2 Nbd7 8.
O-O Be7 9. e3 O-O 10. Qe2 Rc8 11. Nc3 h6 12. e4 Bh7 13. Rd1 Qc7 14. Bf4 Qb6 15.
a4 Nh5 16. Be3 Qc7 17. Ne5 $5 {Here I came up with the following "combination", sacrificing a pawn to force 20...g6.} Nxe5 18. dxe5 Qxe5 19. f4 Qa5 20.
e5 {[%cal Re2h5]} g6 {Black has saved the knight and is even a pawn up, but my aim was now to deliberately keep the bishop on h7 trapped.} (20... Bg6 21. g4 $18) 21. Ne4 Ng7 22. g4 h5 (22... g5 23. Nf6+ Kh8 24. fxg5
hxg5 25. Bxg5) 23. h3 hxg4 24. hxg4 Rfd8 25. Nf6+ Bxf6 26. exf6 Ne8 27. g5 {
Hallelujah. The bishop on h7 is worse than a pawn!} Rxd1+ 28. Rxd1 Rd8 29. Rxd8 Qxd8
30. Bxa7 Nc7 31. Bb6 Qd7 32. Bxc7 Qxc7 33. Qe5 Qxe5 34. fxe5 {[%csl Rh7] This leads to an endgame with the worst bishop of all time. White literally has an extra piece.} Kf8 35. Kf2 Ke8 36. Ke3 Kd7 37. Kd4 Kc7 38. a5
Kb8 39. Kc5 Kc7 40. b4 Bg8 41. b5 cxb5 42. Kxb5 Bh7 {Bonus exercise: How does White win?} 43. Bf1 Bg8 44. Kc5 Bh7 45. Bb5 Bg8 46. Ba4 Bh7 47. Be8 Bg8 48. Kb5 {
Zugzwang.} Kd8 49. Kb6 Kc8 (49... Kxe8 50. Kxb7 {Black's extra bishop is completely useless.}) 50. Bb5 {is the cruelest} (50. Bc6 {would also work} bxc6 51.
Kxc6 Kb8 52. Kd6 Ka7 53. Ke7 Ka6 54. Kf8) 50... Bh7 51. Be2 Bg8 52. Bf3 Kb8 53.
Bxb7 Bh7 54. a6 *

